A||||6||rodmcneill||Vented vs. non vented caps?||||5||
Z||000000||rodmcneill||08-16-2001||01:44 AM||||When I first got my Fiero's one of the first maintenance items I replaced were the radiator caps. I bought mine at Pep Boys using the Stant catalog. The cap listed was a 16lb. vented cap. This was over 2 years ago neither vehicle has cooling problems(both were flushed) The same Stant catalog lists the same vented cap the Fiero uses for most GM applications. Why would the Fiero use a different cap? The cooling systems are basically all the same, they all use overflow tanks. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||08-16-2001||02:09 AM||||NOT vented!<BR>Most of the systems fluid is at a higher level than the cap, therefore non ventted cap.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rodmcneill||08-16-2001||02:41 AM||||Resto guy most of the coolant level is below the cap, radiator,coolant tubes, most hoses, the entire block, probably most of the heads. I guess I don't understand if a system is pressurised(sp) why would you have vented and non-vented caps.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||08-16-2001||06:18 AM||||Mines had vented cap for 4 yrs and 86000 miles with no probs, even stop n go @ 95* with A/C. Never even had to add to it.<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||08-16-2001||07:20 AM||||If you park with the nose down coolant can siphon through the vented cap into the overflow bottle and out of its overflow onto the ground.<P>I witnessed this phenomenon when I had my 85GT jacked way up at the rear to work on the engine.  Coolant steadily seeped out of the overflow bottle vent.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||08-16-2001||07:53 PM||||Well you're on the right track...<P>The majority of coolant is below the cap but the engine does try to keep some above that level. Exactly how much depends on which engine.<P>It's more of a weight problem than siphoning. When the car is tipped nose down, you have a hell of a bunch of weight trying to move forward. Coolant is heavy.<P>The vented cap, compined with the weight of the coolant pushing forward, and even the smallest of leaks, can let the coolant run right out the vented cap. This is because the flow rate is too slow to shut the cap's check valve.<P>The non vented cap prevents this simply because it has a sprung check valve. All the shifting coolant can do is push it tighter against the seal.<P>It can be very dificult to find these often microscopic leaks letting in air. Sometimes the problem is the water pump seal. The seal is ment to keep pressure in. Some seal designs aren't as good at keeping out air under the kind of condition above. Using the non vented cap solves the problem.<P>The 2 caps are otherwise identical. Infact on cars with DEX-COOL, GM recomends all vented caps be replaced with nonvented. (In that case it is inteneded to help reduce coolant contamination...)<P>(Yes, I'm going to be adding this to the coolant article. I haven't had time.)||||reg||1
Z||000006||rodmcneill||08-17-2001||12:06 AM||||Thanks for the info, Ogre.||||reg||1
